    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks for the quick reply. the tech came out to look at the unit but says the mechanical failure is weird. like, everything looks fine on the surface but nothing is actually powering the compressor. its almost like something is just messing with the wiring. is there any common reason for sudden, unexplained electrical failures in these high-end units? we really need to know if this was just a fluke or if something's wrong with the whole fleet.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sudden electrical failures without obvious damage often point to issues like failing relays, blown control boards, or intermittent connections within the electrical harness. Extreme temperature cycling or vibration over time can also cause subtle wiring faults that only manifest under certain conditions. It is crucial to have a qualified refrigeration electrician thoroughly diagnose the power flow, not just the compressor itself. If fleet-wide integrity is a concern, request a diagnostic report outlining the electrical faults found.",
      "source": "target",
